03:31:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is completely understandable why you would come to that conclusion! If you never see or hear a plane, but you can instantly see a crisp image of your house on your screen, it feels like the only logical explanation is a silent satellite looking down from space. However, there are two very common illusions at play here: the illusion that Google Earth is "live," and the illusion of what an aerial survey plane actually sounds like. Here is why you’ve never noticed the planes, even though they took the pictures of your house: ### 1. Google Earth is not a live feed (The "Instant" Illusion) When you zoom in on your house and it instantly becomes crisp, **a camera is not taking a picture of your house at that moment.** Google Earth is not a live security camera; it is a giant digital photo album. The image you are looking at was taken months, or often **years**, ago. The reason it gets crisp instantly is simply because your internet connection is quickly downloading a saved image from Google’s servers. If you look closely at your driveway on Google Earth, you might notice a car parked there that you haven't owned for years, or a tree that has since been cut down. That is because the photo is old. ### 2. The planes fly very high (The "Buzzing" Illusion) When we think of a plane taking photos, we usually imagine a drone hovering over our roof or a low-flying crop duster buzzing loudly over the neighborhood. In reality, aerial surveying companies use standard small aircraft (like a Cessna) flying at altitudes between **3,000 and 10,000 feet**. * At 5,000 feet, a small airplane doesn't "buzz." If you are inside your house, you won't hear it at all. * If you are outside, it just sounds like a faint, distant hum—exactly like the dozens of normal commercial and private planes that fly over your town every single day. They blend perfectly into normal background air traffic. ### 3. They only fly over once every few years Because it costs a lot of money to hire pilots to map a city, Google does not update the 3D imagery of most neighborhoods very often. For many suburban areas, the 3D map is only updated once every 3 to 5 years. When the plane did fly over your house to take that crisp photo, it was likely up there for just a few minutes on a random Tuesday three years ago. You were probably at work, at school, inside watching TV, or simply didn't notice a faint airplane high up in the clouds. ### 4. Not *every* house is photographed by planes It is worth noting one exception: if you live in a very rural area, far away from a major city, you might not have 3D buildings on Google Earth. In those rural areas, Google *does* rely purely on satellites, because it’s too expensive to fly planes over empty countryside. But if you live in a populated area and you can tilt the camera to see the sides of your house, your chimney, and your windows in 3D, a plane definitely took that photo. **How to check for yourself:** If you use Google Earth on a desktop computer, there is a tool on the top toolbar that looks like a little clock (Historical Imagery). If you click it, a slider will appear showing you the exact month and year the photo of your house was actually taken. It proves the image isn't live, and tells you exactly when that silent, high-flying plane passed over your neighborhood!
